---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";

// Practical help for fasting, for the fasting book to point at. A
// newcomer's first Great Lent is the hard one: they know what is not
// allowed and nobody has told them what to actually eat. This page is
// the answer to that, and is built to grow — each section stands alone,
// so another can be added without rearranging anything.

// Grouped the way a person shops rather than by protein content: the
// numbers are per different serving sizes (a cup, an ounce, a block)
// and ranking them against each other would compare unlike things.
// Figures are Theia's, from the USDA, kept exactly as given.
const proteinGroups = [
  {
    group: "Beans & Lentils",
    note: "The backbone of fasting cooking in every Orthodox country.",
    foods: [
      { food: "Lentils", protein: "18 g", per: "per cup" },
      { food: "Chickpeas", protein: "20 g", per: "per cup" },
      { food: "Pinto beans", protein: "15 g", per: "per cup" },
      { food: "Black beans", protein: "14 g", per: "per cup" },
      { food: "Peas", protein: "8 g", per: "per cup" },
    ],
  },
  {
    group: "Soy",
    note: "The most protein for the money, and it takes any flavour you give it.",
    foods: [
      { food: "Tofu", protein: "36 g", per: "per block" },
      { food: "Tempeh", protein: "31 g", per: "per cup" },
      { food: "Edamame", protein: "17 g", per: "per cup" },
    ],
  },
  {
    group: "Nuts & Seeds",
    note: "Easy to keep on hand, and the answer to the mid-afternoon slump.",
    foods: [
      { food: "Pumpkin seeds", protein: "12 g", per: "per cup" },
      { food: "Hemp hearts", protein: "9.5 g", per: "per 3 tablespoons" },
      { food: "Almonds", protein: "7 g", per: "per ¼ cup" },
      { food: "Peanuts", protein: "7 g", per: "per ounce" },
      { food: "Pistachios", protein: "6 g", per: "per ounce" },
      { food: "Chia seeds", protein: "4.5 g", per: "per ounce" },
    ],
  },
  {
    group: "Grains",
    note: "A base that carries protein of its own, unlike white rice.",
    foods: [{ food: "Quinoa", protein: "8 g", per: "per cup" }],
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Keeping the Fast | An Orthodox Table"
  description="Practical help for Orthodox fasting — plant-based protein, and what to actually eat when meat and dairy are set aside."
>
  <main class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12 md:px-6">
    <p class="font-display text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-tan-dark">
      <a href="/table/" class="text-brand-tan-dark no-underline hover:underline">← An Orthodox Table</a>
    </p>
    <h1 class="mt-2 font-display text-3xl text-text-primary md:text-4xl">Keeping the Fast</h1>
    <div class="mt-5 h-px w-24 bg-liturgical-current"></div>

    <p class="mt-6 text-text-primary/85 leading-relaxed">
      The first Lent is the hard one. You have been told what is set aside and
      often nobody has said what to actually eat — and a week of plain rice
      will make anyone wonder whether they can keep this up. You can. It takes
      knowing a few things.
    </p>

    <!-- The chief worry of anyone new to it, answered with numbers. -->
    <section class="mt-10">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl text-text-primary">Where the protein comes from</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 h-px w-16 bg-liturgical-current"></div>
      <p class="mt-4 text-text-primary/85 leading-relaxed">
        This is the first question almost everyone asks, and the answer is that
        it is not difficult — a cup of lentils carries more protein than three
        eggs. Keep two or three of these in the cupboard and the fast stops
        being a problem to solve.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-6 space-y-8">
        {
          proteinGroups.map((group) => (
            <div>
              <h3 class="font-display text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-tan-dark">
                {group.group}
              </h3>
              <p class="mt-1 text-sm italic text-text-secondary">{group.note}</p>
              <table class="mt-3 w-full border-collapse text-sm">
                <tbody>
                  {group.foods.map((row) => (
                    <tr class="border-b border-border-soft">
                      <td class="py-2 pr-4 text-text-primary">{row.food}</td>
                      <td class="w-20 py-2 text-right font-semibold text-text-primary">
                        {row.protein}
                      </td>
                      <td class="w-40 py-2 pl-4 text-text-secondary">{row.per}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))}
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      <p class="mt-5 text-xs text-text-secondary">
        Figures from the USDA. Serving sizes differ between foods — a block of
        tofu is not a cup of lentils — so read each row on its own rather than
        ranking them against each other.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- The single most useful practical thing this page can teach.
         Almost everyone who says they dislike tofu has only met it
         waterlogged, which is a texture problem and a fixable one. A
         catechumen who learns to press and crisp it has gained a
         weeknight replacement for chicken; one who doesn't tends to
         decide the fast means plain rice. The two recipes it points
         at are in the fasting book in full — the method is summarised
         here because somebody reading an introduction should not have
         to open two more pages to find out what to do. -->
    <section id="tofu" class="mt-12 scroll-mt-24">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl text-text-primary">Tofu, and why you may have had it badly</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 h-px w-16 bg-liturgical-current"></div>

      <p class="mt-4 text-text-primary/85 leading-relaxed">
        Most people who say they don't like tofu have only ever eaten it
        straight from the packet. That is not a taste problem, it is a
        texture one. Tofu is sold sitting in water, and until that water
        is out it will not brown, it will not take up a marinade, and it
        stays soft in a way that is genuinely unpleasant to eat. Pressed
        and then crisped, it is a different food — firm, browned at the
        edges, and a real stand-in for chicken or beef in a stir fry, a
        taco or a bowl.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-text-primary/85 leading-relaxed">
        It is worth learning once, early, because it is the difference
        between a fast of plain rice and a fast you can keep.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-8 grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="border border-border-soft bg-cream-card p-5">
          <p class="font-display text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-tan-dark">Step one</p>
          <h3 class="mt-1 font-display text-xl text-text-primary">Press it</h3>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-primary/85">
            Buy <strong>firm or extra firm</strong> — never regular or silken,
            which fall apart. Drain it, then get the water out one of two ways:
          </p>
          <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-primary/85">
            <li>
              <strong>With a press:</strong> tighten a little, stand it over a
              bowl, and give it a few more turns every five minutes, flipping
              it so both sides drain. Tightening hard all at once makes the
              block bulge at the sides.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Without one:</strong> lay the block — or slices, which
              press better — on a folded towel on a board, put another towel
              and board on top, and weigh it down with a cast-iron pan or a
              few tins. Enough weight to squeeze, not enough to crush. Swap the
              towels when they are soaked.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
            Thirty minutes is the minimum. An hour is better, and it can sit in
            the fridge overnight.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-4">
            <a
              href="/table/recipe/?slug=how-to-press-tofu"
              class="font-display text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-blue-dark no-underline hover:underline"
            >
              The full method →
            </a>
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="border border-border-soft bg-cream-card p-5">
          <p class="font-display text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-tan-dark">Step two</p>
          <h3 class="mt-1 font-display text-xl text-text-primary">Crisp it</h3>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-primary/85">
            Cut the pressed block into cubes. Toss them in soy sauce, then in a
            spoonful each of flour and corn starch with a little garlic powder,
            until every side is coated.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-primary/85">
            Get the oil properly hot — <strong>the tofu should sizzle the
            moment it goes in</strong>. Lay the cubes in one layer, leave them
            alone three to five minutes until browned, then turn and brown the
            other side. Crowding the pan steams them instead, which undoes the
            pressing.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
            A non-stick pan or a well-seasoned cast-iron skillet. Salt at the
            end if it needs it.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-4">
            <a
              href="/table/recipe/?slug=crispy-tofu-pan-fried"
              class="font-display text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-blue-dark no-underline hover:underline"
            >
              The full recipe →
            </a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-5 text-sm text-text-secondary">
        Once you have those two, the tofu dishes in the
        <a href="/table/fasting/" class="font-semibold text-brand-blue-dark">fasting book</a>
        — fajitas, tacos, scramble, teriyaki — all start from the same place.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- The thing a website must not get wrong. -->
    <section class="mt-12 border-l-2 border-sacred-gold bg-cream-card p-5">
      <h2 class="font-display text-base text-text-primary">If you are unwell, pregnant, or caring for a child</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-primary/85">
        The fast bends for those it would harm. The Church has always said so.
        Speak with <strong>Fr. Antonios</strong> — not with a website and not
        with a well-meaning parishioner. Children, the sick, the pregnant and
        nursing, the elderly and those doing heavy work are all accounted for,
        and the rule for you is his to give.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="mt-10 border-t border-border-soft pt-6 text-sm text-text-secondary">
      Looking for what today allows? The
      <a href="/calendar/" class="font-semibold text-brand-blue-dark">parish calendar</a>
      says plainly whether it is a fast day and whether fish, wine or oil are
      permitted. Ready to cook?
      <a href="/table/fasting/" class="font-semibold text-brand-blue-dark">Open the fasting book →</a>
    </p>
  </main>
</Layout>
